# Client recipes — {{ dgx_served_models | join(', ') }}

The gateway speaks the OpenAI API, so any OpenAI SDK works with two changes:
point `base_url` at the gateway and use the gateway key instead of a vendor key.

| Setting | Value |
|---|---|
| Base URL | `{{ dgx_gateway_endpoint }}` |
| Model names | {% for name in dgx_served_models %}`{{ name }}`{% if not loop.last %}, {% endif %}{% endfor %} |

The key is not stored in this directory. Read it from the cluster when you
need it:

```sh
kubectl -n {{ dgx_gateway_namespace }} get secret litellm-master \
  -o jsonpath='{.data.master-key}' | base64 -d
```

Files here:

- `client.env` — exports the two variables the examples read.
- `python_example.py` — OpenAI Python SDK, streaming and non-streaming.
- `curl_example.sh` — one request per served model, useful for a quick check.

There is no database configured, so the gateway does not persist spend logs.
Restarting it loses counters; that is deliberate for a single-node box.
